pergunta:
What is the best design pattern to use if I have two entities and each entity has repositories. I have 
@Entity
@Table(name = "doctor_account")
public class DoctorAccount {

    @Id
    private Integer id;

    private Integer specialtyId;

    @Column(unique=true)
    private String phone;

    private String passwordHash;

    private String fullName;

@Entity
@Table(name = "user_account")
public class UserAccount {
    @Id
    Private Integer id;

    @Column(unique=true)
    private String phone;

    private String passwordHash;

    private String fullName;

I have a service layer that takes id as input to get an entity. Id that is in user_account is not in doctor_account and vice versa. Imagine in future new user types will be added, then we need to use design patern for future code changes. What is the best design pattern to use? Give an example of the code. I am using spring framework java, spring data.

resposta:
In this scenario, you might want to consider using the Strategy Pattern. This pattern allows you to define a family of algorithms, encapsulate each one, and make them interchangeable. This would allow you to have different implementations for each entity type while maintaining a single interface.

Here is an example of how you could implement the Strategy Pattern for your entities:

1. Define an interface for your entities:

```java
public interface Account {
    Integer getId();
    String getPhone();
    String getPasswordHash();
    String getFullName();
}
```

2. Create implementations for each entity type:

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "doctor_account")
public class DoctorAccount implements Account {

    @Id
    private Integer id;

    private Integer specialtyId;

    @Column(unique=true)
    private String phone;

    private String passwordHash;

    private String fullName;

    // getters and setters
}

@Entity
@Table(name = "user_account")
public class UserAccount implements Account {
    @Id
    Private Integer id;

    @Column(unique=true)
    private String phone;

    private String passwordHash;

    private String fullName;

    // getters and setters
}
```

3. Define a service that uses the Strategy Pattern to retrieve the correct entity based on the ID:

```java
@Service
public class AccountService {

    private final DoctorAccountRepository doctorAccountRepository;
    private final UserAccountRepository userAccountRepository;

    public AccountService(DoctorAccountRepository doctorAccountRepository, UserAccountRepository userAccountRepository) {
        this.doctorAccountRepository = doctorAccountRepository;
        this.userAccountRepository = userAccountRepository;
    }

    public Account getAccount(Integer id) {
        DoctorAccount doctorAccount = doctorAccountRepository.findById(id).orElse(null);
        if (doctorAccount != null) {
            return doctorAccount;
        }

        UserAccount userAccount = userAccountRepository.findById(id).orElse(null);
        if (userAccount != null) {
            return userAccount;
        }

        return null;
    }
}
```

In this example, the `AccountService` class uses the `findById` method of both the `DoctorAccountRepository` and the `UserAccountRepository` to retrieve the entity with the given ID. If an entity is found, it is returned. If no entity is found, `null` is returned.

This approach allows you to easily add new entity types in the future by simply creating a new implementation of the `Account` interface and adding a new repository for that entity type. The `AccountService` class can then be updated to include the new repository and check for the new entity type.

